{
  "title": "Normal Bone Metabolism",
  "url": "https://www.orthobullets.com/basic-science/9012/normal-bone-metabolism",
  "subtitle": "Updated: Jun 16 2021",
  "sections": [
    {
      "text": "Introduction",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Normal bone metabolism is the complex sequence of bone turnover (osteoclastogenesis) and bone formation (osteoblastogenesis)",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "**Physiology of bone metabolism**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "bone has structural and metabolic functions",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "metabolic functions of bone largely involve the homeostasis of **calcium and phosphate**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "release of calcium, or absorption of calcium, by bone is largely regulated by hormones and, less so, by steroids",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "**Regulators of bone metabolism**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "Hormones",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "PTH",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Calcitonin",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Sex Hormones (eg. estrogen, androgens)",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Growth Hormone",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Thyroid Hormones",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "Steroids",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "Vitamin D",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Glucocorticosteroids",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "**Properties of bone metabolism**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "Bone mass",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "bone mass is the measure of bone tissue present at the end of skeletal maturity",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "represents both its volume and size, as well as the density of the mineralized tissue",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "peak bone mass occurs in hip between ages 16 and 25",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "5",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "greater in men and African Americans",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "Bone loss",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "bone mass decreases by 0.3 to 0.5% per year after skeletal maturity",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "further decreases by 2-3% per year for untreated women during the 6th-10th years after menopause",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "rate of bone loss can be modulated by structural and metabolic factors",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Calcium",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Location",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "**bone (99%)**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "blood and extracellular fluid (0.1%)",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "intracellular (1%)",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Function",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "calcium has a wide range of function including",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "muscle cell contraction",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "nerve conduction",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "clotting mechanisms",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Forms of calcium",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "bone",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "majority is **hydroxyapatite**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "serum",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "Ca++ bound to protein (45%)",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "free-ionized Ca++ (45%)",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "bound to various anions, eg. citrate, bicarbonate (10%)",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Regulation",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "absorption from the **digestive tract**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "resorption from **bone**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "resorption in the **kidneys**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Dietary requirements",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "2000 mg/day for lactating women",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "1500 mg/day for pregnant women, postmenopausal woman, and patients with a healing bone fracture",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "1300 mg/day for adolescents and young adults",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "750 mg/day for adults",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "600 mg/day for children",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Dysfunction",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "hypercalcemia",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "hypocalcemia",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Phosphate",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Location",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "**bone (86%)**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "blood and extracelluar fluid (0.08%)",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "intracellular (14%)",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Function",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "key component of bone mineral",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "important in enzyme systems and molecular interactions",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Forms of phosphate",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "bone",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "majority is **hydroxyapatite**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "serum",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "mostly inorganic phosphate (H2PO4-)",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Regulation",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "plasma phosphate is mostly unbound and reabsorbed by the kidney",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "may be excreted in urine",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "elevated serum phosphate can lead to increased release of PTH and bone resorption",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Dietary intake",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "**1000-1500 mg/day**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "PTH",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Structure",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "84 amino acid peptide",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Origin",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "synthesized and secreted from **chief cells in the four parathyroid glands**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Net effect",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "increases serum calcium",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "decreases serum phosphate",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Mechanism",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "bone",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "PTH stimulates osteoblasts to secrete IL-1, IL-6 and other cytokines to **activate osteoclasts and increase resorption of bone**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "Increases osteoblast production of M-CSF (macrophage colony-stimulating factor) and RANKL, which increases number of osteoclasts.",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "**Paradoxically, osteoclasts do not express receptor for PTH**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "kidney",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "stimulates enzymatic conversion of 25-(OH)-vitamin D3 converted to **1,25-(OH)2-vitamin D3** (active hormone form) which:",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "increases resorption of Ca++ in kidney (increasing serum Ca++)",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "increases excretion of PO4- from kidney (decreasing serum phosphate)",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "intestine",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "no direct action",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "indirectly increase Ca++ absorption by activating **1,25-(OH)2-vitamin D3**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Dysfunction",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "PTH-related protein and its receptor have been implicated in metaphyseal dysplasia",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "**Parathyroid hormone-related protein (PTHrP)** has related effects to PTH as it binds to the same receptors on osteoblasts and renal cells to increase serum calcium",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "4",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Calcitonin",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Structure",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "32 amino-acid peptide hormone",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Origin",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "produced by clear cells in the **parafollicles of the thyroid gland (C cells)**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Net effect",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "limited role in calcium homeostasis",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "inhibit number and activity of osteoclasts",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Function",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "bone",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "inhibits osteoclastic bone resorption by decreasing number and activity of osteoclasts",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "osteoclast have receptor for calcitonin",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "Inc. serum Ca > secretion of calcitonin > inhibition of osteoclasts > dec. Ca (transiently)",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "Dysfunction",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "secreted by medullary thyroid tumors and mulitple endocrine neoplasia type II tumors",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "Recombinant calcitonin used to treat Paget disease, osteoporosis, and hypercalcemia in malignancy",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Vitamin D",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Structure",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "fat soluble secosteroid (steroid with a 'broken ring')",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Origin",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "produced by skin when exposed to sunlight (UV B-generated Vitamin D)",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "dietary intake (lipid-soluble vitamin D3)",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "active metabolite **1,25-(OH)2-vitamin D3 formed by two hydroxylations in the liver and kidney, respectively**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Net effect",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "maintains normal serum calcium levels by activating osteoclasts for bone resorption and increasing intestinal absorption of calcium (increase serum Ca++)",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "**promotes the mineralization of osteoid matrix**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Function",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "liver",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "**activated-vitamin D3** converted to **25-(OH)-vitamin D3**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "kidney",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "25-(OH)-vitamin D3 converted to **1,25-(OH)2-vitamin D3** (active hormone form)",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "activated by",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "text": "increased levels of PTH",
                          "table": null,
                          "images": [],
                          "importance": "2",
                          "children": []
                        },
                        {
                          "text": "decreased levels of serum Ca++, P",
                          "table": null,
                          "images": [],
                          "importance": "2",
                          "children": []
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "**1,25-(OH)2-vitamin D3** (active hormone form)can be inactivated to 24,25-(OH)2-vitamin D3",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "inactivity occurs with:",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "text": "decreased levels of PTH",
                          "table": null,
                          "images": [],
                          "importance": "2",
                          "children": []
                        },
                        {
                          "text": "increased levels of serum Ca++, P",
                          "table": null,
                          "images": [],
                          "importance": "2",
                          "children": []
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "vitamin D parallels that of PTH by increasing reabsorption of Ca in the kidneys",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "bone",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "1,25-(OH)2-vitamin D3 stimulates **terminal differentiation of osteoclasts**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "when osteoclasts mature they do not respond to 1,25-(OH)2-vitamin D3 and respond mostly to cytokines released by osteoblasts",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "**1,25-(OH)2-vitamin D3 promotes the mineralization of osteoid matrix produced by osteoblasts**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Dysfunction",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Vitamin D deficiency causes osteomalacia and rickets",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "phenytoin (dilantin) causes impaired metabolism of vitamin D",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Estrogen",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Structure",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "D ring steroid hormone",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Origin",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "predominantly in the ovaries",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "synthetic forms available",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Net effect",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "prevents **bone loss by decreasing the frequency of bone resorption and remodeling**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Function",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "alone, because bone formation and resorption are coupled, it also indirectly decreases bone formation",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "leads to an increase in bone density of the femoral neck and reduces the risk of hip fracture",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "most important sex-steroid for peak bone mass attainment in both men and women",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Therapeutic estrogen",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "outcomes",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "decreases bone loss if started within 5-10 years after onset of menopause",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "significant side effects so risk/benefit ratio must be evaluated",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "gains in bone mass usually limited to an annual increase of 2-4% for the first 2 years of therapy",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "secondary effects",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "increases risk of",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "**heart disease**",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "4",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "**breast cancer**",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "4",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "decreases risk of",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "**hip fracture**",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "4",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "**endometrial cancer** (if combined with cyclic progestin)",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "laboratory",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "will see a decreases in",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "**urinary pyridoline**",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "**serum alkaline phosphatase**",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Growth Hormone",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Function",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "increases serum calcium by",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "increased absorption in intestine",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "decreasing urinary excretion",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "function is interdependent with insulin, somatomedins, and growth factors (TGF-B, PDGF, mono/lyphokines)",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Gigantism",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "oversecretion or increased response to growth hormone effecting the **proliferative zone** of the growth plate",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Thyroid Hormone",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Function",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "5",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "regulates skeletal **growth at the physis** by stimulating",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "chondrocyte growth",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "type X collagen synthesis",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "alkaline phosphatase activity",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "thyroid hormones increase bone resorption and can lead to **osteoporosis**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "large doses of therapeutic thyroxine can mimic this process and cause osteoporosis",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Steroids",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Function",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "increase bone loss by",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "**decreasing Ca++ absorption** in intestine through a decrease in binding proteins",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "**decreasing bone formation** (cancellous more so than cortical bone) by",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "decreasing collagen synthesis",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "inhibiting osteoblast activity",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
